# Concurrent agents

SCOPE: the vault repo only. These conventions, and the push-to-main default they assume,
must never leak onto a software repository attached to the same session.

Several agents write this vault at once and none can see the others. All any writer shares
is `origin/main`; git's ref update is the only atomic operation available. **Assume the
other agent is not following this rule** — everything below makes one compliant agent safe
alone.

## The write cycle

1. `git pull --no-rebase --no-edit` before reading anything you intend to change.
2. Read the file. Your risk window opens here, closes at push — keep it seconds, not
   minutes; a long job is local work plus one push, never a longer window.
3. Make the smallest edit that satisfies the request.
4. Commit one logical change: `<area>/<note>: <what changed>` — your declaration; none
   other is needed.
5. `git push` — now, not at session end.
6. Rejected? `git fetch`, diff the paths you touched against
   `git diff --name-only $(git merge-base HEAD origin/main)..origin/main`. No overlap →
   `git merge --no-edit origin/main` (clean) → push. Overlap → read `origin/main`'s version
   of each overlapping file first, then resolve by the table below.
7. After any merge, re-read every file you touched before pushing — git merges text, not
   meaning; two agents adding the same fact differently merges clean and leaves it wrong.
   That fix is part of your merge, not a later chore.
8. Rejected three times → stop retrying. Put the content in a **new** note (anywhere; the
   location doesn't matter) and push that instead — a new path can't collide, and nothing is
   stranded when the container is reclaimed. Say so in chat.

**Never declare a claim** — no lock file, no "editing X" marker. A push already is the
compare-and-swap; a claim is a weaker copy of it plus a stale-lock failure mode git doesn't
have. Heartbeats are worse here than anywhere: each refresh is a commit, and every commit
reaches the owner's phone within seconds.

## What a legal edit looks like

- **Smallest unit** — one line, bullet, or field. Never regenerate a section or file from a
  draft in your head. Anchor no longer matches → the file moved under you: re-read and
  retry the targeted edit, never force it through with a rewrite.
- **Never reformat for content** — no re-wrapping, re-ordering, "while I'm here". Reflow
  manufactures conflicts out of edits that would have merged untouched.
- **Never delete a note** unless asked this session; emptying one is deleting it. **Never
  move or rename a note as bookkeeping** — folders carry no semantics here
  (folders carry zero semantics here), so enrichment and a state change happen in place. Superseded content
  is marked `state: done` or `state: dropped` in place, with the dated `## Decisions` entry
  and a `[[wikilink]]` to whatever superseded it. A move/rename is still legal when the user
  asks, clearly implies it, or a genuine reorganisation needs it — never as a side effect of
  any other task.
- **Renames are their own commit** — fix inbound `[[wikilink]]`s in the same commit, push
  alone. git merges a rename cleanly; the cost is links nothing rewrites.
- **Frontmatter: one key at a time, block-style lists.** Block `tags:` collides as two
  additions and resolves by union; flow-style (`tags: [a, b]`) collides as one line and
  can't be resolved without a rewrite. Never reorder keys.
- **New notes get a name nobody else would pick** — a date plus a specific slug.
- **A recent edit is not garbage.** One from the last hour is another session mid-flight —
  don't revert or tidy it unless that's the task.
- Memory files carry reconcile-before-write on top of this — see `agent-memory`.

## Resolving a merge yourself

Auto-resolve **only when the result keeps every line of prose from both sides.** The moment
resolution means choosing between two pieces of writing, it is not yours to choose.
Frontmatter is the one exception, only for the keys named here.

| Collision | Resolution |
|---|---|
| Additions at one anchor, or adjacent | Take both, published side first; drop only exact duplicates |
| Other side is your own earlier commit | Take both; same line, yours wins |
| Different notes created at the same path | Keep the published one; rename yours with a discriminator |
| `updated:` or any timestamp | The later value |
| `state:` | Further-along value (`not-started` < `started` < `done`) |
| `tags:`/`projects:`/`domains:`/`depends_on:` | Union: existing order, then new |
| Any other frontmatter scalar | Keep published; a new note with both values + both SHAs |
| Delete vs. edit | Keep the file (git already left it); a new note recording the deletion |
| Same lines, both substantive prose | Conflicted copy |
| Attachment or binary | Conflicted copy; never merge bytes |
| Dotfolder file, not covered above | Stop, report in chat |
| Rule, `.gitattributes`, `.gitignore`, `.obsidian/*.json` | Stop, report in chat — owner ratifies these |

**The published side keeps the note.** Make the copy in exactly this order — backwards, it
overwrites the note the owner is reading:

    git show :2:<path> > "<name> (conflicted copy $(date -u '+%Y-%m-%d %H.%M.%S'))<ext>"
    git checkout --theirs -- <path>
    git add -- <path> "<the copy>"

In a pull-merge `:2:` is your side, `--theirs` is `origin/main`. Never make the copy under a
dotfolder — Sync doesn't traverse them, so it's visible to agents and no device; route that
collision to "stop, report" instead.

**Small and specific goes into a new note instead.** A conflicted copy freezes the note for
every agent until a human reconciles it — spend one only when both full versions genuinely
need to sit side by side. Never touch a note that already has one: a note and its
conflicted copy are the owner's to reconcile, never an agent's.

## Banned verbs

`push --force[-with-lease]`, `merge -X ours|theirs`, `reset --hard` onto anything pushed,
rebasing pushed commits, `commit --amend` on a pushed commit, `git checkout <sha> -- <path>`
to "restore" over a newer file, and `checkout --ours|--theirs` — except the middle line of
the conflicted-copy procedure above, where the discarded side is already in the copy.

That list is the whole safety story: **an honest merge cannot silently lose a committed
line.** A stale rewrite over another agent's edit conflicts loudly; a deletion racing an
edit leaves the edit in the tree. Every banned verb discards a side without reading it.
Restore lost text by re-applying it as a targeted edit, never by checking out an old SHA.

Before committing, run `git diff --cached --numstat`: a file you didn't create this session
showing more than a handful of removed lines you didn't intend is a stale whole-file
rewrite — re-read, redo as a targeted edit, and say so in chat.
